\subsection{Sensitivity to IMU Lever-Arm Installation}
\label{sec:imu-lever-arm-study}

The preceding validation places the IMU at the vessel center of gravity (CG).
A real installation may instead be displaced by several decimeters, in which
case rotation produces a translational acceleration at the sensor even when
the CG acceleration is unchanged.  For a body-fixed lever arm $r$ the rigid-
body relation is
%
\begin{equation}
  a_{\mathrm{IMU}}
  = a_{\mathrm{CG}}
  + \dot{\omega}\times r
  + \omega\times(\omega\times r),
  \label{eq:imu-lever-arm}
\end{equation}
%
where $\omega$ and $\dot{\omega}$ are expressed in the body frame.  The first
extra term is tangential acceleration and the second is centripetal
acceleration.  Treating $a_{\mathrm{IMU}}$ as $a_{\mathrm{CG}}$ therefore
injects a deterministic, attitude-correlated model error into the same
accelerometer channel used for both attitude and wave acceleration.

To measure this effect without changing any other part of the estimator, the
standard eight JONSWAP and PM--Stokes records are replayed with the IMU moved
\SIlist{10;20;30}{cm} along each canonical body direction: athwartships,
fore--aft, and vertical.  The record's CG motion, attitude, gyroscope,
magnetometer, stochastic sensor realization, OU adaptation, covariance,
pseudo-measurements, startup logic, and vibration guard are unchanged.

The installation and the estimator's model of it sit on opposite sides of the
sensor, and the harness implements them there rather than by pre-processing
records.  The \emph{installation stage} applies \eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm} to the
accelerometer truth before sensor corruption, which is what a sensor at $r$
physically measures; the \emph{model stage} removes the estimator's own
reconstruction of the same term after corruption, immediately before fusion,
which is the stage a firmware implementation owns.  Nothing downstream of that
point knows the IMU is off the CG, so any difference in the scored error is
attributable to the lever arm alone.

Three matched modeling arms share one installation.  In the \emph{unmodeled}
arm no lever-arm model runs and the installation error stands.  In the
\emph{exact-model} arm the lever vector and the record's own angular kinematics
are supplied to the measurement model, so both rotational terms are removed
exactly.  In the \emph{gyro-model} arm the estimator has only what it would
have on hardware: it reconstructs $\dot{\omega}$ from the corrupted rate by a
two-pole low-pass at \SI{15}{Hz} followed by a causal second-order backward
difference, and evaluates \eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm} on the result.  The
exact-model arm is intentionally an oracle bound --- it measures the error
recoverable from correct lever-arm modeling --- while the gyro-model arm
measures what a deployable implementation actually recovers.

