\IfFileExists{ou3_lever_arm_tilt.svg}{%
  \begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\textwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou3_lever_arm_tilt}
    \caption{Pooled maximum roll/pitch RMS for the same sweep, normalized by
    the CG-mounted baseline.  This is the channel the effect reaches first:
    off-CG translational acceleration is absorbed by the attitude solution
    before it appears in displacement, so the athwartships arm roughly doubles
    the pooled tilt error at \SI{30}{cm} while barely moving the 3-D score of
    Fig.~\ref{fig:imu-lever-arm-penalty}.  The fore--aft arm briefly reduces
    pooled tilt at short offsets, which is the injected term happening to
    oppose the residual the estimator already carries there; it is not a
    benefit an installation can rely on, and it reverses by \SI{30}{cm}.}
    \label{fig:imu-lever-arm-tilt}
  \end{figure*}
}{}

\IfFileExists{ou3_lever_arm_mechanism.svg}{%
  \begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\textwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou3_lever_arm_mechanism}
    \caption{The mechanism behind Figs.~\ref{fig:imu-lever-arm-penalty} and
    \ref{fig:imu-lever-arm-tilt}, measured directly on the specific-force
    channel rather than on the score.  Left: RMS of the rotational term
    \eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm} that the installation injects, which grows
    linearly in $|r|$ at fixed sea state.  Right: the fraction of that term
    each modeling arm leaves behind at \SI{30}{cm}.  The unmodeled arm leaves
    all of it by definition, the exact model cancels it identically, and the
    gyro model leaves only the fifth its finite derivative band cannot follow
    --- in every canonical direction alike, since the reconstruction does not
    depend on which direction $r$ points.}
    \label{fig:imu-lever-arm-mechanism}
  \end{figure*}
}{}

\IfFileExists{ou3_lever_arm_sea_state.svg}{%
  \begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\textwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou3_lever_arm_sea_state}
    \caption{Per-sea penalty at \SI{30}{cm}, each sea normalized by its own
    CG-mounted baseline, for the direction that maximizes each channel.  The
    two channels run in opposite directions with sea state: the displacement
    penalty is worst in the mildest seas, where the injected term is largest
    relative to the wave signal, while the tilt penalty is worst in the
    steepest seas.  Pooling over all eight seas, as
    Table~\ref{tab:imu-lever-arm} does, is dominated by the largest and
    therefore understates both.  Both models remove the penalty in every sea.}
    \label{fig:imu-lever-arm-sea-state}
  \end{figure*}
}{}

\IfFileExists{ou3_lever_arm_cutoff.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou3_lever_arm_cutoff}
    \caption{The deployable model's one design parameter.  Too narrow a
    derivative band and the low-pass phase lag misaligns a correction whose
    amplitude is already right, so the model is worse than none; too wide and
    the differentiated gyroscope noise it admits exceeds the term it removes.
    Between the two failure modes the basin is flat, which is what makes the
    deployable model robust rather than tuned.}
    \label{fig:imu-lever-arm-cutoff}
  \end{figure}
}{}

Two conclusions follow.  First, the installation penalty is real at realistic
offsets and it is not uniform across the state.  Attitude degrades well before
displacement does, and the two channels do not even share a worst case: the
displacement penalty peaks in the mildest seas, where the injected term is
largest relative to the wave signal, while the tilt penalty peaks in the
steepest ones.  A single pooled ratio therefore understates the effect in both
directions, which is why Fig.~\ref{fig:imu-lever-arm-sea-state} reports it sea
by sea.

Second, the penalty is entirely deterministic.  The exact-model arm returns to
the CG baseline in every direction, at every offset, and in every sea, so
nothing about an off-CG installation exceeds what \eqref{eq:imu-lever-arm}
describes; there is no residual stochastic cost to pay.  The gyro-model arm
shows that most of that recovery survives having only a noisy, biased rate to
differentiate, provided the derivative band is chosen inside the basin of
Fig.~\ref{fig:imu-lever-arm-cutoff}.  The practical consequence for an
installation is that the lever arm must be measured and modeled, not that the
IMU must be moved to the CG.
